Below is a minimal, self‑contained snippet that uses the **`functions.vOe`** helper to update the `version` field of a `packageInfo` object **only when the new value differs from the current one (or when the field is missing)**.

```js
// 1️⃣  Import the helper (adjust the import path if needed)
import { functions } from './path/to/your/functions/module';

// 2️⃣  Your original object
const packageInfo = {
  name: "my‑awesome‑pkg",
  version: "1.0.0",
  description: "A cool package"
};

// 3️⃣  Desired new value
const newVersion = "1.2.3";

// 4️⃣  Call the helper
const updatedPackageInfo = functions.vOe({
  // Pass the *stringified* object
  r: JSON.stringify(packageInfo),

  // Property name to update
  e: "version",

  // New value – also stringified so the helper sees it as a string literal
  t: JSON.stringify(newVersion)   // → "\"1.2.3\""
});

// 5️⃣  Parse the result back into a JS object
const finalPackageInfo = JSON.parse(updatedPackageInfo);

console.log(finalPackageInfo);
/*
  {
    name: "my‑awesome‑pkg",
    version: "1.2.3",
    description: "A cool package"
  }
*/
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why |
|------|--------------|-----|
| 1 | Import the helper | Makes the `vOe` function available. |
| 2 | Define `packageInfo` | Your original data. |
| 3 | Set `newVersion` | The value you want to apply. |
| 4 | Call `functions.vOe` | The helper receives the object, the property name, and the new value (all as strings). It checks: <br>• If the property doesn’t exist → it adds it.<br>• If the property exists **and** the new value is different → it updates it.<br>• If the property exists **and** the value is the same → it leaves the object unchanged. |
| 5 | Parse the returned JSON | Convert the updated JSON string back into a usable JavaScript object. |

> **Tip:**  
> If you’re working in a Node.js environment that supports top‑level `await`, you can wrap the call in an async function and `await` the result if `functions.vOe` returns a promise.

That’s all you need to safely bump the `version` field while respecting the “only if different” rule. Happy coding!
